# Loop Engineering

Turn a vague "keep working on this until it's done" request into a fully specified,
autonomous, self-terminating loop: **elicit → plan → approve → wire an engine →
execute → report**.

Why the ceremony up front: autonomous loops without explicit, machine-checkable
completion criteria either run forever, silently drift off-goal, or declare victory
without evidence. Five minutes of configuration prevents hours of wasted iterations.
Never skip the elicitation and approval phases, even if the user's prompt seems
complete — confirm the inferred configuration instead of asking from scratch.

## The one lesson that shapes everything: loops die after iteration 1

A model's natural behavior is to complete one visible unit of work, summarize, and
end its turn. In a loop, **a completed iteration is not a completed task** — the
task is the converged loop. If continuation depends on the model "remembering" to
keep going, the loop will almost always stop after the first pass. So continuation
must be **mechanical**, provided by an execution engine chosen in Phase 1.

**Engine selection is intent-first.** "Autonomous" does not mean "interactive":
when the user's intent is *unattended* operation — run by itself to completion,
overnight, walk away, 無人值守 — the default is Engine B. Engine A automates turns
*inside an open, attended session*; it is not background work.

- **Engine B — external driver script** (Ralph-style; **default for unattended
  intent**): a bash `while` loop invokes a fresh headless agent per iteration; the
  script decides whether to go again by reading `.loop/state.json`. Survives under
  `nohup`/`tmux` with the terminal closed, works in Claude Code, Codex,
  Antigravity, or any headless-capable harness. This is the walk-away engine.
- **Engine A — Claude Code `/goal`** (hands-free **while you watch**): after every
  turn, a Stop-hook evaluator checks the condition and, if the loop should
  continue, starts exactly one more turn. It advances **one turn at a time**, only
  while this client stays open, the goal is active, and tool calls are
  auto-approved (Auto mode — a hard prerequisite, not an optimization). It is
  **not** a background job: closing the session stops the loop, and stopping a
  misbehaving loop ultimately relies on the human (`/goal clear`). Choose it for
  attended or semi-attended runs.
- **Engine C — in-session self-loop** (last resort): the model chains iterations
  inside its own turns under strict continuation discipline. Weakest by design;
  use only when A and B are both unavailable.

**Design constraint to respect throughout: assume the executing model has a limited
context window and may be a smaller model.** All durable state lives in files, not
in memory. Engines A and B both exploit this: the files are the loop's memory
spine; any single turn or process can die without losing the loop.

## Anatomy: goal → criteria → verification → three exits

A loop is fully specified when four layers exist, each more machine-checkable
than the one above:

- **Ultimate goal** — the north star; one sentence of *why* (plan §1).
- **Completion criteria** — the goal's machine-checkable projection: *what
  "done" means* as commands, metrics, plateaus, or capped human checkpoints
  (plan §2). Multi-item goals get a `.loop/backlog.json` work queue whose
  "all items pass" check becomes the criterion.
- **Verification** — the procedure that produces *evidence* every iteration;
  this is the loop's **backpressure**. The faster the cheap tier, the tighter
  the loop; the full tier gates convergence (plan §3).
- **Stop conditions** — a loop stops in exactly one of three ways, and all
  three are designed before iteration 1:
  1. **Converged** — criteria pass, evidence printed.
  2. **Ceiling** — iteration budget, wall clock, per-iteration timeout, turn
     cap (plan §6). Ceilings are the *primary* safety mechanism: the only
     exit that cannot be reasoned around or gamed.
  3. **Escape hatch** — structured early exits (`blocked`, `stalled`,
     `paused`, `aborted`; plan §7 + execution.md) for walls the loop should
     report, not grind against.

  A loop with only a success exit cannot stop when the goal is unreachable; a
  loop with only ceilings burns the whole budget failing silently; a loop
  without hatches turns every wall into wasted iterations.

## Artifacts

All loop state lives in a `.loop/` directory at the project root (created in
Phase 2). If there is no project directory, use the current working directory.

```
.loop/
├── LOOP_PLAN.md          # the approved configuration — single source of truth
├── policy.json           # machine-readable permissions + scope (from plan §4/§5)
├── state.json            # run state: status, stop_reason, iteration, metrics, git origin
├── iterations/           # structured per-iteration records NNNN.json (machine-readable)
├── journal.md            # append-only human-readable log with verification evidence
├── backlog.json          # optional multi-item work queue [{id,title,passes,…}] — append-only
├── LESSONS.md            # append-only one-line signs every later iteration must obey
├── check_scope.py        # scope enforcement helper (from assets/check-scope-template.py)
├── run_loop.sh           # Engine B only: the driver (from assets/run-loop-template.sh)
├── ITERATION_PROMPT.md   # Engine B only: the fixed per-iteration prompt
└── FINAL_REPORT.md       # written on termination (Engine B: by the driver; A/C: in Phase 4)
```

Transient operator files (all engines honor them): `touch .loop/STOP` — clean
manual abort at the next check; `.loop/STEER.md` — a one-shot mid-run
directive the next iteration reads, quotes into the journal, and deletes (it
steers within the plan; it cannot expand scope or permissions).

`LOOP_PLAN.md` is immutable during a run except through the amendment protocol in
`references/execution.md`. Treat it the way you would treat a signed spec.

## Phase 0 — Detect environment and check for resume

1. **Resume check.** If `.loop/state.json` exists with `status` `"running"` or
   `"paused"`, a previous loop was interrupted. Read the plan, the state, and the
   journal tail; summarize where it stopped; ask the user: resume, restart fresh
   (archive to `.loop-archive-<timestamp>/`), or abandon. If it exists with a
   terminal status (`converged`, `stalled`, `aborted`, `max_iterations`,
   `blocked`), confirm archiving the old `.loop/` to
   `.loop-archive-<timestamp>/` before starting fresh — a new run must never
   mix its `iterations/` and `journal.md` with a previous run's. Never
   silently start a new loop over an old one.

2. **Interaction mode for elicitation.** If an interactive question tool exists
   (Claude Code: `AskUserQuestion`), use it for the plan-mode-style option picker.
   Otherwise fall back to numbered plain-text questions with lettered options and
   an explicit "or type your own answer" line, batched into one message.

3. **Engine availability signals** (facts only — the *recommendation* is
   intent-first and happens in Phase 1):
   - The driver script needs a shell, `python3`, and an agent CLI that runs
     headless (`claude -p ...`, `codex exec ...`, or equivalent).
   - `/goal` needs Claude Code ≥ v2.1.139, an accepted workspace trust dialog,
     hooks not disabled, Auto mode for unattended turns, and a session that stays
     open. You cannot always verify version from inside the session — let the
     user confirm.

4. **Task tracking.** If a todo tool exists, create entries for the phases.

## Phase 1 — Elicitation

Read `references/elicitation.md` now — full question bank, per-dimension suggested
options, batching rules. Summary of the rules:

1. **Depth mode first**: quick (infer-heavy, ≈3 confirm calls) vs thorough
   (every dimension asked, no call cap). Honor standing preferences from user
   memory/CLAUDE.md silently; detect "越詳細越好"-type intent → thorough;
   otherwise ask as the first question.
2. **Pre-fill to sharpen questions, not to skip them**; every plan value
   carries a provenance tag — `(stated)` verbatim in the prompt, `(asked)`,
   or `(inferred)`. Detect **unattended intent** ("自己跑到完", "過夜",
   "無人值守", "跑完叫我", overnight, walk away) → Engine B becomes the
   recommended option in dimension 9.
3. **Must-ask dimensions are never inferred, in either direction**: network
   access, package install, destructive operations, git remote/branch depth,
   and the final deny_paths list. Absence of mention licenses a question,
   never a value; conservative values may appear only as recommended options
   inside a question. The permissions matrix gets its own dedicated call.
   This outranks every batching guideline.
4. Every question ships with 2–4 concrete, task-specific options plus free
   input, with one recommended default and a one-line reason.
5. Cover all **nine dimensions**: goal, completion criteria, verification
   method (including who checks — maker–checker), tool permissions (must-ask
   matrix), scope in/out, iteration budget + stall detection, failure/stall
   policy, checkpointing + autonomy, **execution engine** (intent-first).
6. **Completion criteria must be machine-checkable** (command exit code, metric
   threshold, plateau rule) or explicitly converted to human-checkpoint criteria
   with a hard cap. Never accept "you'll know when it's done".
7. **Maker–checker**: the entity that judges completion should not be the entity
   that did the work, wherever possible. Prefer a verification command whose
   output cannot be gamed; Engine A adds an independent evaluator; offer a
   verifier-subagent option if the harness has subagents.
8. Ask in the user's language; keep technical terms in English.

## Phase 2 — Plan and approval

1. Create `.loop/` and write `LOOP_PLAN.md` from `assets/loop-plan-template.md`.
   Fill every field with its provenance tag (`stated`/`asked`/`inferred`);
   write `none` rather than leaving blanks. Plan §9 **must** include the
   **Crank line**: one sentence stating exactly who starts each next
   iteration, what must stay true for that to keep happening, and what the kill
   switch is. This line exists because wrong expectations about the crank are how
   users end up feeling deceived by "autonomous" loops.
2. **Inferred-confirmation gate.** Before asking for overall approval, render
   a separate "**Inferred, not asked**" block: every `(inferred)` value with a
   one-line consequence (e.g. "no network → literature survey impossible
   mid-loop"), and have the user confirm or modify these item by item.
   Must-ask dimensions may never appear in this block — if one does, that is
   an elicitation bug: go back to Phase 1 and ask. Only low-risk leftovers
   belong here.
3. **Paste the complete plan into the conversation** and ask: approve, or change?
   When presenting, restate the Crank line in plain words (e.g. Engine A: "這個
   loop 由 /goal 的 evaluator 驅動,一次推進一個 turn;只在這個 client 開著、Auto
   mode 開啟時前進;它不是背景工作;kill switch 是 /goal clear"). Apply requested
   changes, re-render, repeat until explicit approval. Silence or a topic change
   is not approval.
4. On approval, write `.loop/policy.json` from plan §4/§5, create
   `.loop/iterations/`, copy `assets/check-scope-template.py` to
   `.loop/check_scope.py`, create an empty `.loop/LESSONS.md`, seed
   `.loop/backlog.json` when plan §2 defines one (the run appends items and
   flips `passes` with evidence — it never deletes or rewords an item),
   record the git origin (branch + HEAD sha) and, when plan §8 uses git
   checkpointing, create and switch to `loop/<slug>` from that clean HEAD
   (git checkpoint protocol; dirty tree → pause and ask), and initialize
   `state.json` (atomic writes from here on — tmp file, then rename):
   ```json
   {"status": "running", "stop_reason": null,
    "iteration": 0, "max_iterations": <N>, "stall_threshold": <K>,
    "best_metric": null, "iterations_since_improvement": 0,
    "git": {"origin_branch": "<name|none>", "origin_head": "<sha|none>",
             "loop_branch": "loop/<slug>|none"},
    "started_at": "<ISO8601>", "updated_at": "<ISO8601>"}
   ```
   write the plan-approved header into `journal.md`, then **wire the engine**:

   - **Engine B**: instantiate `.loop/run_loop.sh` from
     `assets/run-loop-template.sh` — edit its CONFIG block defaults to plan
     §9's values (AGENT_CMD_JSON, PROMPT_MODE, MAX_WALL_MINUTES,
     ITERATION_TIMEOUT_MINUTES, SLEEP_SECONDS; integers, no unit suffix) —
     and `.loop/ITERATION_PROMPT.md` from
     `assets/iteration-prompt-template.md`, replacing every `<placeholder>`
     from the plan. `chmod +x .loop/run_loop.sh`. Tell the user how to launch —
     **pilot one iteration first** (`MAX_LAUNCHES=1 bash .loop/run_loop.sh`,
     watch it write a record, append the journal, and commit; this shakes out
     headless permission/auth bricking before bed), then `nohup`/`tmux` for
     true walk-away runs — and that rerunning the script later resumes from
     `state.json`.
   - **Engine A**: **hard gate first** — confirm with the user that Auto mode
     will be ON and the client will stay open for the whole run; if their intent
     is actually unattended, stop and recommend Engine B instead. Then compose
     the goal condition using the STOP-framing template in
     `references/execution.md` §Engine A (criteria inlined verbatim, the
     LOOP STATUS clause spelled out as a STOP signal, a turn cap; ≤4,000 chars),
     store it in plan §9, and hand the user the exact command:
     `/goal <condition>` — or headless `claude -p "/goal <condition>"`.
   - **Engine C**: no wiring; proceed to Phase 3 under the continuation
     discipline in `references/execution.md` §Engine C.

Do not execute a single loop action before approval — this gate is the point.

## Phase 3 — Execution

Read `references/execution.md` before the first iteration. The per-iteration
protocol (reload state → one hypothesis → execute → verify verbatim → record →
decide) is identical for all engines; **the engine only determines who starts the
next iteration**:

- **Engine B**: one iteration per process. The script restarts you; your job ends
  at the `LOOP STATUS:` line. You have no memory of prior iterations — the files
  are your memory.
- **Engine A**: one iteration per turn. Ending the turn is *correct* — the
  evaluator restarts it while the session lives. End every turn with a
  `LOOP STATUS:` line.
- **Engine C**: nothing restarts you. After a "continue" decision, the next action
  **in the same response** is Step 1 of the next iteration. Ending your message
  while `status` is `"running"` kills the loop.

Non-negotiable rules (full rationale in execution.md): evidence or it didn't
pass; never weaken a criterion to pass it; never touch out-of-scope paths or
disallowed tools — pause instead; one change-set per iteration; print
verification output to the conversation, not only the journal; and never
print a status you cannot evidence — a false `converged` to end the loop is
the one unforgivable move.

## Phase 4 — Termination and report

Set `status` to `converged | max_iterations | stalled | aborted | blocked`
with its matching `stop_reason` (see references/execution.md §State schema),
then report: outcome with evidence, per-criterion pass/fail table, trajectory
(iterations, metric progression, best iteration), leftovers and suggested next
steps — in the conversation and in `.loop/FINAL_REPORT.md` (under Engine B
the driver already wrote a machine summary there; extend it, don't discard
it). For non-converged outcomes, offer: extend budget, amend plan, accept
best-so-far, or roll back.

## Cross-environment notes

- **Feature-detect, never assume.** `/goal`, question tools, todo tools, git, and
  subagents are all optional. Hard requirements: read/write files, run shell
  commands, converse.
- **Other harnesses (Codex, Antigravity, …)**: Engine B is the portable path —
  set the driver's `AGENT_CMD_JSON` to that harness's headless command. Engine C is
  the in-session fallback everywhere.
- **No git?** Checkpoint by copying changed files to `.loop/checkpoints/iter-<N>/`.
- **Small-model discipline.** When unsure mid-loop, re-read `LOOP_PLAN.md` and
  `references/execution.md` instead of improvising. About to do something the
  plan doesn't cover → that is the pause signal, not a judgment call.
- **Language.** User-facing prose follows the user's language; `.loop/` field
  names, `state.json` keys, status values, and the `LOOP STATUS:` line stay in
  English exactly as templated so scripts and later sessions can parse them.

